{
  "paraId": "6F385B86",
  "style": "",
  "text": "In this study, serum vitamin D levels were found to mediate between DI and frailty. Vitamin D affects mineral metabolism homeostasis and also possesses anti-inflammatory and antioxidant properties [40, 41]. It further regulates cell cycle processes (division, proliferation and apoptosis), thereby inhibiting angiogenesis [42]. Specifically, vitamin D modulates the inflammatory microenvironment by inhibiting pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., TNF-α, IL-6) and upregulating anti-inflammatory factors (e.g., IL-10) [43, 44]. We found that individuals with lower serum vitamin D levels had a significantly higher frailty risk with higher DII, suggesting diet-induced chronic inflammation may exacerbate the debilitating process through vitamin D deficiency. Consistent with previous studies, the vitamin D receptor (VDR) is widely expressed in immune cells, and its deficiency impairs negative feedback regulation of inflammatory signaling [45], causing muscle atrophy, bone density loss and impaired nerve function and ultimately a debilitating syndrome [46, 47]. Mechanistically, chronic low-grade inflammation drives bone-muscle degeneration via osteoclast activation and inhibited muscle protein synthesis [48, 49], whereas vitamin D breaks this vicious cycle through calcium-phosphorus regulation and anti-inflammatory effects [50]."
}

